  "verdict": {
    "VDR_increase_helps": false,
    "optimal_VDR": "V0 (VDR=2, 1 iqs slice per call) is optimal; all higher VDRs are slower or equal",
    "trip_reduction_valid_lever": false,
    "summary": "VDR increase (more iqs-slices per vec_dot call) does NOT help on gfx1101 IQ3_XXS. The per-trip fixed cost (loop overhead, address compute) is small (~5 insns out of 417 = 1.2%), so amortizing it is negligible. The per-trip work scales linearly with VDR (8*vdot4 per call), but the per-row total work is unchanged. D1 has 16 vdot4 per call vs V0's 8, so each call is 2.19x longer (913 vs 417 insns) but trip count halves (17 -> 9), giving 1.16x more total insns per row. D2 and D3 scale worse. The fused gateup path is more sensitive because the 2x weight-matrix reads dominate; D3's occupancy drop (44 vs 64 blocks/CU) and VGPR spike (113) further degrade performance.",
    "why_not_works": [
      "Per-trip fixed cost is small (1.2% of 417 insns in V0); amortization gives no win",
      "Per-trip work scales linearly with VDR; total per-row insns increases with VDR",
      "Independent accumulators expose ILP, but the 8-deep v_dot4 chain is still 8-deep per iqs-slice, so the chain depth is unchanged within each slice",
      "For V0: 8-deep chain into v1; For D1: 2 independent 8-deep chains into v1 and another accumulator. The 2 chains can issue in parallel, but the SIMD pipe is already saturated by the existing chain"
    ],
    "subagent_a_alignment": "Subagent-A's ISA analysis identified the 8-deep v_dot4 chain as the binding serialization. Splitting into 2 4-deep chains (their top recommendation) is different from VDR increase, which is 2 8-deep chains into independent accumulators. VDR increase does not reduce chain depth per slice; it only reduces trip count. The subagent-A recommendation is for chain-depth reduction, not trip-count reduction."
  }
